{"benchmark": "ifeval", "task_id": "1087", "sample_idx": 22, "model_name": "stockmark/stockmark-2-100b-instruct", "content": "the social network is a compelling biographical drama directed by andrew kevin vincent, adapted from the book by ben mezrich. the film focuses on the early days of facebook and the life of its founder mark zuckerberg, portrayed by jesse eisenberg in an award-winning performance. the story delves into the legal disputes and personal relationships that shaped the creation of the world's largest social media platform.\n\nvisually, the movie is stunning, with a sleek and modern style that effectively captures the essence of silicon valley and the digital age. the cinematography is top-notch, utilizing vibrant colors and dynamic camera angles to bring the story to life. the score by thomas newman is haunting and evocative, enhancing the emotional depth of the narrative.\n\nthe performances in the film are outstanding. jesse eisenberg delivers a nuanced portrayal of zuckerberg, capturing both his brilliance and his flaws. the supporting cast, including andrew garfield as Eduardo Saverin and james mcavoy as william hoffman, provide solid performances that add layers of complexity to the story. the film explores themes of ambition, betrayal, and the cost of success, making it both entertaining and thought-provoking.\n\nwhile the film takes some creative liberties with the true events, it remains a powerful commentary on the intersection of technology, law, and human relationships. the social network is a must-watch for anyone interested in the origins of facebook or the broader implications of social media on society. the script is sharp, the direction is masterful, and the performances are memorable, making this a truly exceptional film.", "logprobs": [{"token": "the", "logprob": -0.16175192594528198}, {"token": " social", "logprob": -0.008759880438446999}, {"token": " network", "logprob": -0.0005490464391186833}, {"token": " is", "logprob": -0.0933